course: religion-ethics
lesson: 43
title: Sharing and fairness
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Sharing means letting others use or have part of what you have, and it builds friendship; but fairness asks a harder question: who should get what, and why?
explain: Picture two children and one last slice of cake: one fair way is "one cuts, the other chooses", because the one who cuts will try hard to make both halves the same.
explain: People have different ideas of fairness: equal shares for everyone, more for those who need more, or more for those who worked harder; a good answer asks which idea fits the situation.
explain: Christians believe God gives gifts to share; the first Christians shared what they had so that no one was in need (Acts 4:32-35), and Jesus praised a poor widow who gave two small coins.
explain: For a child today this means taking turns, not leaving anyone out, picking teams kindly, and thinking about people far away who have much less than we do.
fact: Sharing means letting others use or have part of what you have.
fact: You do not have to share everything; some things are personal or special, but kindness and fairness should guide you.
fact: "One cuts, the other chooses" is fair because the cutter has every reason to make the two pieces equal.
fact: It is kind to share toys with a visiting friend, and it is also fair to put one very special toy away safely before they come.
fact: There are three main ideas of fairness: equal shares, shares by need, and shares by effort; people disagree about which fits best (disputed).
fact: When a team wins a prize and one member did most of the work, some say share equally as a team and some say share by effort.
fact: A fair turn-taking system gives everyone a go, the same length of time, and is known to everyone before it starts.
fact: Leaving someone out of a game on purpose hurts; ask who is left out and why.
fact: A group can sometimes have a good reason to limit a game, such as a full team, but not a reason that is unkind to one person.
fact: When captains always pick the same children last, fairer ways include numbering off, drawing names, or a teacher making the teams.
fact: The first Christians shared their belongings so that no one among them was in need (Acts 4:32-35).
fact: Jesus praised a poor widow who gave two tiny coins, because she gave all she had; he said she had given more than all the rich people who gave out of their spare money (Mark 12:41-44).
fact: Many faiths and humanists teach that the rich should share with the poor; Christians give to charity, Muslims give zakat and Sikhs share food in the langar.
fact: People disagree whether giving to charity is a kindness you choose or a duty you owe (disputed); many Christians say it is both.
exercise: What does sharing mean? | Letting others use or have part of what you have | Giving away everything you own to anyone who asks | Swapping things only when you get something better | Lending things only to people who are your best friends
exercise: Do you have to share absolutely everything? | No, some things are personal, but be kind and fair | Yes, keeping anything for yourself is always selfish | No, you never need to share anything that is yours | Yes, but only with people who have shared with you first
exercise: Why is "one cuts, the other chooses" a fair way to split the last slice of cake? | The cutter will make both pieces equal, or they get the smaller one | The cutter always gets the bigger piece as a reward for cutting | The chooser has to take whichever piece is put nearest to them | It is not fair, because the older child should always decide
exercise: A friend is coming to play. Is it fair to put one very special toy away first? | Yes, you share the rest kindly but may keep one special thing safe | No, a guest must be allowed to play with every single thing you own | Yes, and you should hide all your toys so none get broken | No, putting away any toy shows you do not like your friend
exercise: Your team won a prize, but one member did most of the work. What is a well-reasoned view? | People disagree: some say equal shares, some say by effort | The one who did most work should get it all, and the rest nothing | Equal shares are always right, and nobody should ever disagree | The team captain should keep the prize, because captains are in charge
exercise: Which three ideas of fairness do people use? | Equal shares, shares by need, and shares by effort | Biggest first, oldest first, and loudest first | Shares by luck, shares by age, and shares by height | Shares for friends, shares for family, and none for others
exercise: A class gives out extra reading help. Which idea of fairness fits best? | Shares by need, because the children who struggle need it most | Equal shares, so everyone gets exactly the same help | Shares by effort, so only the children who work hardest get any help | Shares by luck, so names are drawn out of a hat
exercise: What makes a turn-taking system on the swings fair? | Everyone gets a go of the same length, and the rule is known before | The biggest children go first and stay as long as they like | Whoever runs fastest to the swings keeps them all playtime | The teacher's favourites go first and the others wait
exercise: Why is waiting your turn a sign of respect? | It shows that other people's wants count as much as yours | It shows that you are far too shy to ask for a go first yourself | It shows that you do not really want a go at all | It shows that the rules matter more than people do
exercise: A group of friends will not let one child join their game. What is the best question to ask? | Who is left out, and is it fair? | Is the child who wants to join good enough at the game? | Will joining the game make the group less popular? | Does the child have a toy the group could borrow?
exercise: Can a group ever have a good reason not to let someone join? | Yes, if the game is full, but never for an unkind reason | No, a group must always let everyone join at once, whatever the game | Yes, if the child is new or different from the others | Yes, if the group just does not like that child much
exercise: Captains always pick the same children last. What is a fairer way? | Number off or draw names, so nobody is publicly chosen last | Let the captains pick, because the best players deserve to win | Let the children picked last form their own team every time | Let the loudest children decide who plays on each team
exercise: How did the first Christians share, according to Acts 4? | They shared so that no one was in need | They kept everything and only shared on Sundays | They gave all their money to the Roman emperor | They shared food only with people from their own family
exercise: Why did Jesus praise the poor widow who gave two small coins? | Because she gave all she had, which was more than the rich gave | Because two coins was the largest gift anyone gave at the temple that day | Because she was the first person to give money at the temple | Because she asked for her coins back afterwards
exercise: Should rich countries and people share with poor ones? What is a well-reasoned answer? | Many faiths and humanists say yes, because everyone has equal worth and some have far too little | No, because poor people in other countries are poor only because they are lazy | Yes, but only with countries that can pay them back later | No, because what happens far away is never our business
exercise: Is giving to charity a kindness or a duty? | Many Christians say it is both a free kindness and a duty | It is only a kindness, so nobody ever needs to think about it | It is only a duty, so people who give feel no kindness at all | It is neither, because charity does not really help anyone
exercise: Which faith practice is an example of sharing with those in need? | The Sikh langar, a free meal served to everyone | A birthday party that only invited friends may attend | A shop that sells food at the normal price | A club that charges a large fee to join
test: Your brother grabs the bigger half of the last brownie and says, "I cut it, so I pick, them's the rules." How do you put him right? | The fair rule is one cuts and the other chooses, so you pick first | He is right, the person who cuts should always get first pick | The older child always gets the bigger piece, so that's fine | Brownies should never be shared, so neither of you gets one
test: Mia says, "Josh's mum is coming with Josh, and I'm hiding my new tablet. Is that selfish?" What is the best answer? | It's fair to keep one special thing if she shares the rest | Yes, hiding anything at all makes her a terrible friend | No, and she should hide every toy she owns as well | Yes, she must give Josh the tablet to keep to show she cares
test: A boy at football yells, "Oi, Sam's always picked last cos he's rubbish, deal with it." What is the best-reasoned reply? | Being picked last hurts; drawing names is fairer | He's right, rubbish players should always be picked last | Tell him he's an idiot and kick the ball at him | Say nothing, because picking teams has nothing to do with fairness
test: Three kids won a science prize of 30 pounds; Ruby did most of the work. Her friend says, "Equal split, end of." What does a judge want to hear? | There are reasons for equal and for effort shares; talk it over | Equal split, end of, because that is the rule in every situation | Ruby should take it all, because the others did nothing useful | The teacher should keep the money, since nobody can agree
test: A classmate who is not religious asks, "Do you have to be religious to think sharing is right?" What is the best answer? | No, humanists and people of every faith and none teach sharing | Yes, only religious people believe in sharing | Yes, people without a religion are never generous | No, because sharing is not really a good thing anyway
test: Your sister moans, "Why should I share my sweets with Ben? He never shares his." What is the best-reasoned answer? | Share because it is kind, not only to get something back | You should never share with Ben, because he deserves nothing | You must share all your sweets, every single time, forever | Take his sweets when he isn't looking, so it's fair
test: The girls at break say, "You can't play, it's our game, go away." There's plenty of room. What is the best response? | Calmly say there's room for one more, and tell a teacher if being left out keeps happening | Accept it quietly, because groups can always leave out anyone they like for any reason | Shout that they are all stupid and ruin their game | Pretend you didn't want to play, and never speak to them again
test: Your friend says, "The widow gave only two coins, so she was the least generous." How do you put him right? | Jesus said she gave the most, because it was all she had | He is right, the size of a gift is all that matters | She gave nothing at all, the coins were for the temple guard | Jesus said she should have kept the coins for herself
test: Dad says, "The computer's for everyone, but your brother's on it all evening, sod it." What is a fair fix? | A timetable with equal turns that everyone knows in advance | Whoever gets to the computer first can stay as long as they want | Ban everyone from the computer for good | Let the oldest child have it every evening
test: A judge at the olympiad asks: "If one child is hungry and another is not, should food be shared equally?" What is the best-reasoned answer? | Sharing by need, because the hungry child is still hungry | Yes, always equal, because fairness can only mean exactly the same | No, the one who is not hungry should get it all to save it | It depends only on which child is more popular in the class
test: A Muslim friend says, "We give zakat every year, part of our savings to the poor." What is the best response? | That's like Christian giving to charity; many faiths teach sharing with the poor | That's not real charity, only Christians give properly | That's a waste, because the poor will never use money well | That's a secret tax, so it doesn't count as sharing
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the procedure whereby one party divides an item and the other selects a portion? | A method of division which secures fairness, the divider being induced to make the portions equal | A method of division which confers the larger portion upon the divider invariably | A method of division wherein the selector is compelled to accept the nearer portion | A method of division permissible solely between persons of identical age
exam: Identify the three principal conceptions of fairness in the distribution of goods. | Distribution in equal shares, distribution according to need, and distribution according to effort | Distribution according to age, distribution according to height, and distribution by chance | Distribution to friends, distribution to relatives, and distribution to none | Distribution to the strongest, the loudest, and the earliest to arrive
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the early Christian community as described in Acts 4:32-35. | Possessions were held in common in order that no member should be in want | Possessions were surrendered in their entirety to the Roman authorities | Possessions were distributed exclusively among the apostles' own families | Possessions were sold and the proceeds retained by the wealthiest members
exam: Given that a group excludes a child from a game in which places remain available, ascertain the response most consistent with sound ethical reasoning. | The exclusion is unjustified, no fair reason existing and harm being done to the child | The exclusion is justified, a group being entitled to exclude whomsoever it pleases | The exclusion is justified, provided that the child is unfamiliar to the group | The exclusion is of no moral consequence, games being trivial matters
